{:edb/db-before-t 2449 :edb/db-after-t 2450 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535762 50 #inst "2026-10-14T22:16:44.990Z" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186054779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527188 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186063600 1078 #edb/ref 17592186063602 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186064259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527206 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186064315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527146 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186064342 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527218 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186064343 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527209 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186064358 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527167 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186064924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186064924 1071 "$45÷0=$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186064924 1071 "What is $45 \\div 0$?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186064924 1074 "We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $45÷0$ is undefined." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068577 1071 "Factor $x^3 - 8$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068577 1071 "Factor $x^3 - 8.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068577 1074 "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2)\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the given expression as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 8 = x^3 - 2^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the given expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^3 - 8 & = x^3 - 2^3 \\\\[3pt] & = (x - 2)\\left(x^2 + 2\\cdot x + 2^2\\right) \\\\[3pt] & = (x - 2)\\left(x^2 + 2x + 4\\right) \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068577 1074 "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the given expression as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 8 = x^3 - 2^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the given expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nx^3 - 8 & = x^3 - 2^3\\[3pt]\n\n& = (x - 2)\\left(x^2 + 2\\cdot x + 2^2\\right)\\[3pt]\n\n& = (x - 2)\\left(x^2 + 2x + 4\\right)\t\t\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068578 1071 "Factor $8n^3 + m^6$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068578 1071 "Factor $8n^3 + m^6.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068578 1074 "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3+b^3=(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2)\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a sum of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n8n^3 + m^6 = (2n)^3 + (m^2)^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8n^{3} + m^{6} &= (2n)^{3} + (m^{2})^{3} \\\\\n&= (2n + m^{2})((2n)^{2} - 2n \\cdot m^{2} + (m^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (2n + m^{2})(4n^{2} - 2m^{2}n + m^{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068578 1074 "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3+b^3=(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a sum of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n8n^3 + m^6 = (2n)^3 + (m^2)^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{n}^{3}+{m}^{6} & =(2n)^{3}+({m}^{2})^{3} \\\\  & =(2n+{m}^{2})((2n)^{2}-2n\\cdot {m}^{2}+({m}^{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =(2n+{m}^{2})(4{n}^{2}-2{m}^{2}n+{m}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068579 1071 "Factor $1 - \\dfrac 1 {t^9}$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068579 1071 "Factor $1 - \\dfrac 1 {t^9}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068579 1074 "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n1 - \\dfrac{1}{t^9} = 1^3 - \\left(\\dfrac{1}{t^3}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - \\frac{1}{t^{9}} &= 1^{3} - (\\frac{1}{t^{3}})^{3} \\\\\n&= (1 - \\frac{1}{t^{3}})(1^{2} + 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{t^{3}} + (\\frac{1}{t^{3}})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 - \\frac{1}{t^{3}})(1 + \\frac{1}{t^{3}} + (1)/((t^{3})^{2})) \\\\\n&= (1 - \\frac{1}{t^{3}})(1 + \\frac{1}{t^{3}} + \\frac{1}{t^{6}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068579 1074 "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\na^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n1 - \\dfrac{1}{t^9}  = 1^3 - \\left( \\dfrac{1}{t^3}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{9}} & ={1}^{3}-{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})({1}^{2}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{3})^{2}}) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068650 1071 "![](images/06/06a54157969bfc559c8622512bca59b796f8100f8b52542165acb1eb2f397585.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068650 1071 "![](images/06/06a54157969bfc559c8622512bca59b796f8100f8b52542165acb1eb2f397585.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068650 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x}{:}\n$$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph.\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants.\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty$, though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty$, though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nOf the given options, only graph I has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068650 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}{:}$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph.\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants.\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty,$  though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nOf the given options, only graph I has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068651 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$?\n\n1. The point $(64,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$\n2. The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in[-1,\\infty)$\n3. $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068651 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. The point $(64,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$\n\nII. The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in[-1,\\infty)$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068651 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(64)=\\sqrt[3]{64}=4$.\n- Statement II is false since $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is defined for $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186068651 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(64)=\\sqrt[3]{64}=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false since $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is defined for $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527139 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527213 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527122 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527151 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527225 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527226 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527135 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527192 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527181 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527185 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068970 1050 660.0 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186068981 1050 420.0 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186069923 1064 "To verify the identity\n\n$$\na^3 + b^3 = (a + b)(a^2 - ab + b^2)\n$$\n\nwe begin by expanding the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n(a + b)(a^2 - ab + b^2) = a(a^2 - ab + b^2) + b(a^2 - ab + b^2)\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the distributive law to each term:\n\n- For the first term, we have\n$a(a^2 - ab + b^2) = a^3 - a^2b + ab^2$.\n- For the second term, we have\n$b(a^2 - ab + b^2) = a^2b - ab^2 + b^3$.\n\nAdding both expressions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(a + b)(a^{2} - ab + b^{2}) &= a^{3} - a^{2}b + ab^{2} + a^{2}b - ab^{2} + b^{3} \\\\\n&= a^{3} - a^{2}b + ab^{2} + a^{2}b - ab^{2} + b^{3} \\\\\n&= a^{3} + ab^{2} - ab^{2} + b^{3} \\\\\n&= a^{3} + ab^{2} - ab^{2} + b^{3} \\\\\n&= a^{3} + b^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can verify the formula for the difference in cubes, i.e.\n\n$$\na^3 - b^3 = (a - b)(a^2 + ab + b^2)\n$$\n\nin a similar way." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186069923 1064 "To verify the identity \n\n$$\na^3 + b^3 = (a + b)(a^2 - ab + b^2)\n$$\n\nwe begin by expanding the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n(a + b)(a^2 - ab + b^2) = a(a^2 - ab + b^2) + b(a^2 - ab + b^2)\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the distributive law to each term:\n\n- For the first term, we have\n\n$$\na(a^2 - ab + b^2) = a^3 - a^2b + ab^2.\n$$\n\n- For the second term, we have\n\n$$\nb(a^2 - ab + b^2) = a^2b - ab^2 + b^3.\n$$\n\nAdding both expressions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}) & ={a}^{3}-{a}^{2}b+a{b}^{2}+{a}^{2}b-a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3} \\\\  & ={a}^{3}-\\cancel{{a}^{2}b}+a{b}^{2}+\\cancel{{a}^{2}b}-a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3} \\\\  & ={a}^{3}+a{b}^{2}-a{b}^{2}+{b}^{3} \\\\  & ={a}^{3}+\\cancel{a{b}^{2}}-\\cancel{a{b}^{2}}+{b}^{3} \\\\  & ={a}^{3}+{b}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can verify the formula for the difference in cubes, i.e. \n\n$$\na^3 - b^3 = (a - b)(a^2 + ab + b^2)\n$$\n\nin a similar way." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186069967 1064 "A polynomial of the form $a^3 + b^3$ is called a **sum of cubes**, while a polynomial of the form $a^3 – b^3$ is called a **difference of cubes**.\n\nBoth of these polynomials follow a similar factor pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{3}+b^{3} &= (a+b)(a^{2}-ab + b^{2}) \\\\\na^{3}-b^{3} &= (a-b)(a^{2}+ab + b^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOne trick to memorizing the signs in these patterns is to use the mnemonic **SOAP**, which stands for **S**ame, **O**pposite, **A**lways **P**ositive.\n\n- The sign of the linear factor is the **S**ame as that of the original polynomial,\n- the first sign of the quadratic factor is the **O**pposite sign, and\n- the last sign of the quadratic factor is **A**lways **P**ositive." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186069967 1064 "A polynomial of the form $a^3 + b^3$ is called a **sum of cubes**, while a polynomial of the form $a^3 – b^3$ is called a **difference of cubes**. \n\nBoth of these polynomials follow a similar factor pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{3}+{b}^{3} & =(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}) \\\\ {a}^{3}-{b}^{3} & =(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOne trick to memorizing the signs in these patterns is to use the mnemonic **SOAP**, which stands for **S**ame, **O**pposite, **A**lways **P**ositive.\n\n- The sign of the linear factor is the **S**ame as that of the original polynomial,\n\n- the first sign of the quadratic factor is the **O**pposite sign, and\n\n- the last sign of the quadratic factor is **A**lways **P**ositive." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186069984 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nNotice that we included both positive and negative input values since the cube root is defined over all real numbers.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/06/06fb1e18af5c6069d8d0ca0d4ac2e7c3d8103f7b3e672091d05727323ff0d248.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$.\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- Its range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty$, though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty$, though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nIt is also worth noting that\n\n$$\nf(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nis an invertible function, and its inverse is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = x^3, \\qquad x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nand its inverse are shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cb78384fd1ce682758df76ac9b014b105de080b5923df2b313598b8fc7eae46.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186069984 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$  First, we create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nNotice that we included both positive and negative input values since the cube root is defined over all real numbers.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/06/06fb1e18af5c6069d8d0ca0d4ac2e7c3d8103f7b3e672091d05727323ff0d248.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty,$  though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nIt is also worth noting that $f(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an invertible function, and its inverse is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = x^3, \\qquad x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $f(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x}$ and its inverse are shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0cb78384fd1ce682758df76ac9b014b105de080b5923df2b313598b8fc7eae46.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186072584 1078 #edb/ref 17592186072586 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186073028 1078 #edb/ref 17592186073031 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186073880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527191 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186073899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527128 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186073899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527138 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186073900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527153 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186073900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527168 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186074391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186074391 1071 "There are $18$ parrots and $15$ ducks in a zoo. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\n> The ratio of parrots to ducks is $6: 0$ because there are $6$ parrots for every $0$ ducks." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186074391 1071 "There are $18$ parrots and $15$ ducks in a zoo. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of parrots to ducks is $6:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $6$ parrots for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ ducks." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186074391 1074 "Since there are $18$ parrots and $15$ ducks, the ratio of parrots to ducks is $18:15.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,18 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,15 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,6 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $6:5$ is equivalent to $18:15.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of parrots to ducks is $6:5\\,$ because there are $6$ parrots for every $5\\,$ ducks.\"\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $5.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186074465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186074465 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(4, 2)$ and $(4, 8)$?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186074465 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(4,2)$  and $(4,8)?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186074465 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/66/66c988b7fede0e97eae46388dd0e57aee2c49a3c185c9a888277da8fefc947ab.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](images/63/63f70c20c918dae51ecddc7891a8cbee7c5749720521e62fe26e9bc4e33bb9e6.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/d3/d3693b8c3d07636143b2c77c3bd2645015885b77ec4e8ddffde2e121da0511bc.png)\n\nThe point $(4,8)$ is at a distance of $8$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(4,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $8-2=6.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186078507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527207 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186078611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527161 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105475 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given that the first term is $-1$ and the eighth term is $13$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186105475 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given that the first term is $-1$ and the eighth term is $13.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105475 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is $a_1=-1$, so we just need to figure out the common difference $d$.\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_1=-1$ and $a_8=13$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{8} - a_{1}}{8 - 1} \\\\\n&= (13 - (-1))/(8 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{7} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] & = -1 + (n-1) (2) \\\\[3pt] & = -1 + 2n - 2 \\\\[3pt] & = 2n - 3. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186105475 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is $a_1=-1,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_1=-1$ and $a_8=13$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{1}}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-(-1)}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ta_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = -1 + (n-1) (2) \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = -1 + 2n - 2 \\[3pt]\n\t\t& = 2n - 3.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105476 1071 "The fifth and sixth terms of an arithmetic sequence are $46$ and $50$, respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186105476 1071 "The fifth and sixth terms of an arithmetic sequence are $46$ and $50,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105476 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$.\n\nSince the terms $a_5=46$ and $a_6=50$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{6} - a_{5} \\\\\n&= 50 - 46 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_5=46$, to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_5$ is $4$ jumps after $a_1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_5 &= a_1 + 4d \\\\[3pt] 46 &= a_1 + 4\\cdot 4 \\\\[3pt] 46 &= a_1 +16 \\\\[3pt] 46-16 &= a_1 \\\\[3pt] a_1&= 30. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\[3pt] & = 30 + (n-1) \\cdot 4 \\\\[3pt] & =30 + 4n -4 \\\\[3pt] & = 4n +26. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186105476 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nSince the terms $a_5=46$ and $a_6=50$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{6}-{a}_{5} \\\\  & =50-46 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_5=46,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_5$ is $4$ jumps after $a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_5 &= a_1 + 4d \\[3pt]\n46 &= a_1 + 4\\cdot 4\\[3pt]\n46 &= a_1 +16 \\[3pt]\n46-16 &= a_1\\[3pt]\n      a_1&= 30.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ta_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = 30 + (n-1) \\cdot 4 \\[3pt]\n \t\t& =30 + 4n -4 \\[3pt]\n\t\t& = 4n +26.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105477 1071 "An arithmetic sequence has the $3$rd term $a_{3}=9$ and the $8$th term is $a_{8}=69$. Find a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186105477 1071 "An arithmetic sequence has the $3$rd term $a_{3}=9$ and the $8$th term is $a_{8}=69.$ Find a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105477 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out what the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$ are.\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_{8}=69$ and $a_{3}=9$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{8} - a_{3}}{8 - 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{69 - 9}{8 - 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{60}{5} \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_{3}=9$, to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after $a_1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{3} &= a_{1} + 2d \\\\\n9 &= a_{1} + 2 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n9 &= a_{1} + 24 \\\\\na_{1} &= -15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= -15 + (n - 1) \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&=-15 + 12n - 12 \\\\\n&= 12n - 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186105477 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out what the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$ are.\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_{8}=69$ and $a_{3}=9$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{3}}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{69-9}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{5} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_{3}=9,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after $a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}+2\\cdot 12 \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}+24 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-15+(n-1)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-15+12n-12 \\\\  & =12n-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527176 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527129 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527130 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527133 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527223 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527142 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527197 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186105984 1050 780.0 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186107127 1064 "When $C$ is a closed curve, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" $C.$ It is usually easy to determine which region is inside or outside an arbitrary closed curve using our natural intuition.\n\nOne important quantity that we often need when dealing with closed, oriented curves is the **outward-pointing unit normal vector,** usually denoted $\\mathbf n$.\n\nAs the name suggests:\n\n- $\\mathbf n$ is normal (i.e., perpendicular) to the curve $C$ at *every* point,\n\n- it is a unit vector (i.e., its magnitude equals $1$), and\n\n- it always points in the outward direction.\n\nSince $\\mathbf n$ must be normal to $C$ at *every* point, it must vary as we move around $C.$ Therefore, it is usually written either as a function of the parameter space (i.e., $\\mathbf n(t)$) or as a function of the coordinates of the points on $C$ (i.e., $\\mathbf n(x,y)$). \n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the closed, positively oriented ellipse $C,$ parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n![](images/fe/fea4c20453e9c80493f5c5431859fd7918d75cbaa494ad0b3deeccacfae24019.png)\n\nFor convenience, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/c7/c7d514dbfdb746d94487a7ebca6b239173b854010eeccb80b4afc06cd09a9c0e.svg)\n\nLet's calculate the vector $\\mathbf n$ for this curve. To do this, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA typical tangent vector for our curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10ae867f682158bce2098234210206d4d7eb811be780505ca1984fb9e9516a27.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/27/27e11f86245d4331e33cec34742a7b6c395ff22fe95bcf465ec569f7168b4c0f.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t),$ given by\n\n![](images/00/006458079641036b283371fb36a94b39b11dcb09d796175a555539287218d8a7.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ca9a18d78783a923b63fcc41a8bc41ccab5c4a12bb00f5c305e35e2e1bc40e5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ in an outward-pointing normal vector, but it is not a unit vector. Therefore, we need to normalize it.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(3\\sin t)^2 + (2\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}}\n$$\n\ngives us an outward-pointing unit normal vector to every point on our ellipse." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186107127 1064 "When $C$ is a closed curve, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" $C.$ It is usually easy to determine which region is inside or outside an arbitrary closed curve using our natural intuition.\n\nOne important quantity that we often need when dealing with closed, oriented curves is the **outward-pointing unit normal vector,** usually denoted $\\mathbf n$.\n\nAs the name suggests:\n\n- $\\mathbf n$ is normal (i.e., perpendicular) to the curve $C$ at *every* point,\n\n- it is a unit vector (i.e., its magnitude equals $1$), and\n\n- it always points in the outward direction.\n\nSince $\\mathbf n$ must be normal to $C$ at *every* point, it must vary as we move around $C.$ Therefore, it is usually written either as a function of the parameter space (i.e., $\\mathbf n(t)$) or as a function of the coordinates of the points on $C$ (i.e., $\\mathbf n(x,y)$). \n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the closed, positively oriented ellipse $C,$ parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n![](images/fe/fea4c20453e9c80493f5c5431859fd7918d75cbaa494ad0b3deeccacfae24019.png)\n\nFor convenience, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/c7/c7d514dbfdb746d94487a7ebca6b239173b854010eeccb80b4afc06cd09a9c0e.svg)\n\nLet's calculate the vector $\\mathbf n$ for this curve. To do this, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA typical tangent vector for our curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10ae867f682158bce2098234210206d4d7eb811be780505ca1984fb9e9516a27.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/27/27e11f86245d4331e33cec34742a7b6c395ff22fe95bcf465ec569f7168b4c0f.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t),$ given by\n\n![](images/00/006458079641036b283371fb36a94b39b11dcb09d796175a555539287218d8a7.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ca9a18d78783a923b63fcc41a8bc41ccab5c4a12bb00f5c305e35e2e1bc40e5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ in an outward-pointing normal vector, but it is not a unit vector. Therefore, we need to normalize it.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(3\\sin t)^2 + (2\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}}\n$$\n\ngives us an outward-pointing unit normal vector to every point on our ellipse." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186107129 1064 "Let's consider a general, closed curve $C$ in the plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + y(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume that $C$ is oriented counterclockwise. Our goal is to derive a general formula for $\\mathbf n.$ \n\nAs always, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/48/48517c1b1d9915c8f739f8a3979778eb7ea8ddebe01f276034f8df1c301ce802.svg)\n\nTo calculate $\\mathbf n,$ we follow our usual three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/49/492e23c319bc0681cf1c65d0ff91fccdde02686891b3ae1c1daf9f512f38a7e7.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector \n![](images/e4/e44acf1d3bdb1caaf25b89faa191a58faa7979881d9a7093ec23ea9ab8405103.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(x'(t))^2 + (y'(t))^2}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) \\\\ -{x}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\sqrt{({x}^{′}(t))^{2}+({y}^{′}(t))^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186107129 1064 "Let's consider a general, closed curve $C$ in the plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + y(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume that $C$ is oriented counterclockwise. Our goal is to derive a general formula for $\\mathbf n.$ \n\nAs always, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/48/48517c1b1d9915c8f739f8a3979778eb7ea8ddebe01f276034f8df1c301ce802.svg)\n\nTo calculate $\\mathbf n,$ we follow our usual three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/49/492e23c319bc0681cf1c65d0ff91fccdde02686891b3ae1c1daf9f512f38a7e7.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector \n![](images/e4/e44acf1d3bdb1caaf25b89faa191a58faa7979881d9a7093ec23ea9ab8405103.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(x'(t))^2 + (y'(t))^2}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{′}(t) \\\\ -{x}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\sqrt{({x}^{′}(t))^{2}+({y}^{′}(t))^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186107164 1064 "To find the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms, we must usually apply the following:\n\n- The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nwhere $a_n$ is the $n$th term, $a_1$ is the first term, and $d$ is the common difference.\n\n- We can find the common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms using\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{a_m-a_k}{m-k}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_m$ is the $m$th term, and $a_k$ is the $k$th term.\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186107164 1064 "To find the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms, we must usually apply the following:\n\n- The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by\n$a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d$\nwhere $a_n$ is the $n$th term, $a_1$ is the first term, and $d$ is the common difference.\n- We can find the common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms using\n$d = \\frac{a_m-a_k}{m-k}$\nwhere $a_m$ is the $m$th term, and $a_k$ is the $k$th term.\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186110674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527150 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114518 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114519 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114520 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114521 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114522 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526551 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526600 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526709 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526713 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186114517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526825 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186114517 1078 #edb/ref 17592186526713 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186115096 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y = -x^3$?\n\n![](images/ba/babbe4306b4a068c96f9dc820af3b02a6e25d5779928108f3d783dc0684fdf27.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186115096 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y = -x^3?$\n\n![](images/ba/babbe4306b4a068c96f9dc820af3b02a6e25d5779928108f3d783dc0684fdf27.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186115096 1074 "First, remember that for $x>0$, the graph of $y=-x^3$ decreases very rapidly as we increase $x$. We say that $y \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$. The only graphs that exhibit this decreasing property are III and IV.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs III and IV. To do this, let's evaluate $y=-x^3$ at some value of $x$, say $x=1$. Substituting $x=1$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny = -(1)^3 = -1\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(1,-1)$. Only graph III satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph III corresponds to the graph of $y=-x^3$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186115096 1074 "First, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y=-x^3$ decreases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y \\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty.$ The only graphs that exhibit this decreasing property are III and IV.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs III and IV. To do this, let's evaluate $y=-x^3$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=1.$ Substituting $x=1$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny = -(1)^3 = -1,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(1,-1).$ Only graph III satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph III corresponds to the graph of $y=-x^3.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186115097 1071 "Sketch the graph of $y=x^3+2$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186115097 1071 "Sketch the graph of $y=x^3+2.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186115097 1074 "Since we have\n\n$$\ny=x^3{\\color{blue}\\,+\\,2}\n$$\n\nwe need to translate the graph of $y=x^3$ *up* by $\\color{blue}2$ units.\n\nSo, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=x^3+2\n$$\n\nlooks as follows:\n\n![](images/54/54030b86feaed3fd9b3e818654dae831f4456159d6ae32f5e3619b6f0e1369a7.png)"