 \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 72^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $72^\\circ$.\n\nThen, we recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/64/6479f08add76d919644c8eba7df6bb1d273fd1de808d4af58876a6a5da539fcb.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186119568 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi} 5\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t72^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ is equivalent to $72^\\circ.$ \n\nThen, we recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/64/6479f08add76d919644c8eba7df6bb1d273fd1de808d4af58876a6a5da539fcb.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186119569 1071 "![](images/f0/f0347081906740a955f8a192baa53c65536b110df9adc6021288c43767c9ac2f.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt 3, 1)$, calculate the angle $\\theta$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186119569 1071 "![](images/f0/f0347081906740a955f8a192baa53c65536b110df9adc6021288c43767c9ac2f.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt 3, 1),$ calculate the angle $\\theta.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186119569 1074 "First, we can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/3e/3e5ebde9ee4132e0bd64cb2aaf2a3763abc2b0de1a798e87ed4ee652ca360078.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 3} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3} \\right)} = 30^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186119569 1074 "First, we can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/3e/3e5ebde9ee4132e0bd64cb2aaf2a3763abc2b0de1a798e87ed4ee652ca360078.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 3} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan{\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3} \\right)} = 30^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186119570 1074 "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1dcf96192164d9e1845207773d4fe9596ff3fd0d5fa7e83984260004e8bc32aa.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186119570 1074 "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1dcf96192164d9e1845207773d4fe9596ff3fd0d5fa7e83984260004e8bc32aa.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186119751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527165 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186119752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527127 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186119753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527203 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186119753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527204 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186119753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527215 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186119754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527194 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120042 1050 420.0 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120317 1071 "![](images/fb/fb2876d08dc2aa17b42d137b6d1a486447995e7d52edd37846e9c040265dc1bc.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(1, \\sqrt{3})$, calculate the angle $θ$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186120317 1071 "![](images/fb/fb2876d08dc2aa17b42d137b6d1a486447995e7d52edd37846e9c040265dc1bc.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(1,\\sqrt{3}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120317 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/d4/d4aa760fae46877c78d0bb2b87b7b047832a2600258401af1d021f693bb788b3.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}={60}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120337 1071 "How is the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120337 1071 "How is the angle $\\frac{π}{6}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186120337 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is equivalent to ${30}^{∘},$ which lies in the first quadrant.\n\nNow, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/53/53bdd11d4204beb60f0b705693ed4d0d75f6a3e26bc3a593b0147852be4baa97.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120491 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120491 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $\\frac{7π}{6}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186120491 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={210}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ is equivalent to ${210}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/87/87ee1ce243769647af34f58491087ffab742616993396ff32b0499f667bcedd1.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120493 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $260^{∘}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186120493 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of ${260}^{∘}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186120493 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/eb/ebfef5a8de67bcae7354922ed8c92ef84dee832022b4eb7fcda07e595bd801ac.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186121003 1064 "The unit circle turns out to be a really useful tool to help us to understand the trigonometric functions. However, sometimes, we'd like to represent an angle in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle.\n\nThankfully, the standard way that we represent angles in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle is pretty much the same as with the unit circle!\n\nFor example, the angles $45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ$, and $315^\\circ$ can be represented in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/31/317690d0489b8a5f5cd9e360c9d5894a539908b00ffc50ae0b70bfe03708162b.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186121003 1064 "The unit circle turns out to be a really useful tool to help us to understand the trigonometric functions. However, sometimes, we'd like to represent an angle in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle.\n\nThankfully, the standard way that we represent angles in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle is pretty much the same as with the unit circle! \n\nFor example, the angles $45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ,$ and $315^\\circ$ can be represented in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n  \n\n![](images/31/317690d0489b8a5f5cd9e360c9d5894a539908b00ffc50ae0b70bfe03708162b.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186121004 1064 "The **unit circle** is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/13/138322240b05231ce56ca195a06724da8a1c05ec4701ac1d9d49cadc9129c8c0.png)\n\nThe unit circle is an essential tool for understanding trigonometry.\n\nSuppose that we want to construct an angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane. There is a standard way in which we do this.\n\n- First, we draw the unit circle.\n- Then, we represent $\\theta$ as a central angle of the unit circle. The angle has its vertex at the origin, its initial side lies on the positive $x$-axis, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nFor example, we represent the angles\n\n$$\n\\theta = 45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $315^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d4419633933ba2b5874873f625ca9016d42c2dc9a685c9db3619daf8512fde5.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186121004 1064 "The **unit circle** is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the coordinate plane. \n\n![](images/13/138322240b05231ce56ca195a06724da8a1c05ec4701ac1d9d49cadc9129c8c0.png)\n\nThe unit circle is an essential tool for understanding trigonometry. \n\nSuppose that we want to construct an angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane. There is a standard way in which we do this.\n\n- First, we draw the unit circle.\n\n- Then, we represent $\\theta$ as a central angle of the unit circle. The angle has its vertex at the origin, its initial side lies on the positive $x$-axis, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nFor example, we represent the angles $\\theta = 45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ,$ and $315^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d4419633933ba2b5874873f625ca9016d42c2dc9a685c9db3619daf8512fde5.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186124462 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527143 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186124463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527163 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186125074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123044 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186125078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123068 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186129318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527172 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186133735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527125 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186133735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527199 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186133736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527123 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186133736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527154 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186133737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527179 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186133737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527182 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186147595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527137 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186151727 1078 #edb/ref 17592186151731 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186152870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186152870 1071 "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\n1. $1$\n2. $-2$\n3. $-\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186152870 1071 "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $1$\n\nII. $-2$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186152870 1074 "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $1$ and $-2$ are integers, but $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186157424 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527148 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186159572 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159574 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186159632 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159637 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186161681 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = 3\\ln\\theta + 4\\ln(1-\\theta),\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$ from this sample?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186161681 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n$l(\\theta) = 3\\ln\\theta + 4\\ln(1-\\theta)$,\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$ from this sample?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186161681 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$, we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(3\\ln θ + 4\\ln (1 - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{θ} + \\frac{4}{1 - θ} \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{θ} + \\frac{4}{θ - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{θ} + \\frac{4}{θ - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(3(θ - 1) + 4θ)/(θ(θ - 1)) &= 0 \\\\\n3(θ - 1) + 4θ &= 0 \\\\\n7θ - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n7θ &= 3 \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{3}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} = \\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186161681 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta.$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\theta }+\\frac{4}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\theta }+\\frac{4}{\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\theta }+\\frac{4}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3(\\theta -1)+4\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 3(\\theta -1)+4\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 7\\theta -3 & =0 \\\\ 7\\theta  & =3 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} =  \\dfrac{3}{7}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186161682 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables $X_i\\sim N(\\theta,1)$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = -6\\ln(2\\pi)-8.4 + 12.1\\, \\! \\theta - 6\\ \\! \\theta^2,\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$ for this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186161682 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables $X_i\\sim N(\\theta,1)$ is\n$l(\\theta) = -6\\ln(2\\pi)-8.4 + 12.1\\, \\! \\theta - 6\\ \\! \\theta^2$,\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$ for this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186161682 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$, we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(-6\\ln (2π) - 8.4 + 12.1θ - 6θ^{2}) \\\\\n&= 12.1 - 12θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12.1 - 12θ &= 0 \\\\\n12θ &= 12.1 \\\\\n&= θ \\mid \\approx 1.008\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} \\approx 1.008\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186161682 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta.$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-8.4+12.1\\,\\,\\theta -6\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =12.1-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.1-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =12.1 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈1.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} \\approx 1.008,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162070 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527178 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162115 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527180 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162115 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527184 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162115 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527193 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162115 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527200 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162116 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527147 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162116 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527160 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162116 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527166 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162116 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527212 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162116 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527214 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527187 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527227 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527186 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527217 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162132 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527144 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162132 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527222 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162134 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527202 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162136 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527171 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162136 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527195 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162205 1050 420.0 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162298 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}cxy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le y<1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162298 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}cxy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le y<1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186162298 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y<1\\}.$\n\n![](images/01/01f3a8d99dd38ce2a728fe2cb4e4ab8de933ccd9ba27c87174779c428c303f48.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=x}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}-\\frac{c}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162298 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y<1}.$\n\n![](images/01/01f3a8d99dd38ce2a728fe2cb4e4ab8de933ccd9ba27c87174779c428c303f48.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=x}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}-\\frac{c}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186162300 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{x}^{2}y,\\, & {x}^{2}\\le y<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162300 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c{x}^{2}y,\\, & {x}^{2}\\le y<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186162300 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y<1\\}.$\n\n![](images/b9/b9cfac7f0f82088900ab5e603588a73d4932f08f592321c797a1fd94b8bafb4e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[c{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{14})]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4c}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4c}{21}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162300 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y<1}.$\n\n![](images/b9/b9cfac7f0f82088900ab5e603588a73d4932f08f592321c797a1fd94b8bafb4e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[c{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{14})]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4c}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4c}{21}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186162395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162395 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ),\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162395 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n$l(θ) = 5\\ln θ + \\ln (1 - θ)$,\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $θ^(^)$ from this sample?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186162395 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }+\\frac{1}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }+\\frac{1}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\theta }+\\frac{1}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5(\\theta -1)+\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 5(\\theta -1)+\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 6\\theta -5 & =0 \\\\ 6\\theta  & =5 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{5}{6}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162405 1071 "Find $E[X^{3}(Y + 1)]$, given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {\\frac{y}{x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\le x \\le 3, 1 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186162405 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}(Y+1)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186162405 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{3}(y+1).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}(Y+1)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4y(y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{3}+8)-(\\frac{4}{3}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186163265 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, X_3$ drawn from a population where $X_i\\sim B(5,\\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown probability of success. Let's assume we conducted our sample and got the following data:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the log-likelihood function for this sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = \\ln 125 + 6 \\ln \\theta +9\\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nOur goal is to form an estimate for the unknown parameter $\\theta$. To do this, we find the value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the probability of getting the observed sample data. In other words, we need to maximize the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$. And, since the natural logarithm is an increasing function, a value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ will also maximize the log-likelihood function $l(\\theta)$.\n\nEstimates of an unknown parameter $\\theta$ obtained by maximizing the likelihood function (or, equivalently, the log-likelihood function) are called **maximum likelihood estimates**. We'll use the usual $\\hat{\\theta}$ notation to denote a maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/00/005367bc78937c5bd5b684cb260aff951de2e85d7c2907bb6e11c312ad4f24bd.png)\n\nTo find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$, we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\ln 125 + 6\\ln θ + 9\\ln (1 - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{θ} + \\frac{9}{1 - θ} \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{θ} + \\frac{9}{θ - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6}{θ} + \\frac{9}{θ - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(6(θ - 1) + 9θ)/(θ(θ - 1)) &= 0 \\\\\n6(θ - 1) + 9θ &= 0 \\\\\n15θ - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n15θ &= 6 \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{6}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} = \\dfrac{6}{15}\n$$\n\n**Note**: It's easy to check that this is a maximum of $l(\\theta)$ by calculating the second derivative. We'll omit this step." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186163265 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, X_3$ drawn from a population where $X_i\\sim B(5,\\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown probability of success. Let's assume we conducted our sample and got the following data:\n\n$$\nx_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the log-likelihood function for this sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = \\ln  125 + 6 \\ln \\theta +9\\ln(1-\\theta).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to form an estimate for the unknown parameter $\\theta.$ To do this, we find the value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the probability of getting the observed sample data. In other words, we need to maximize the likelihood function $L(\\theta).$ And, since the natural logarithm is an increasing function, a value of $\\theta$ that maximizes the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ will also maximize the log-likelihood function $l(\\theta).$\n\nEstimates of an unknown parameter $\\theta$ obtained by maximizing the likelihood function (or, equivalently, the log-likelihood function) are called **maximum likelihood estimates**. We'll use the usual $\\hat{\\theta}$ notation to denote a maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta.$ \n\n![](images/00/005367bc78937c5bd5b684cb260aff951de2e85d7c2907bb6e11c312ad4f24bd.png)\n\nTo find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta.$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡125+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{9}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{9}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{9}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{6(\\theta -1)+9\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 6(\\theta -1)+9\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 15\\theta -6 & =0 \\\\ 15\\theta  & =6 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{6}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} =  \\dfrac{6}{15}.$ \n\n**Note**: It's easy to check that this is a maximum of $l(\\theta)$ by calculating the second derivative. We'll omit this step." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186163361 1064 "Let's derive a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimator for the unknown parameter $\\theta$ for a set of $n$ independent and identically distributed Bernoulli random variables.\n\nSuppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{n}$ is an I.I.D random sample, where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{n}$, the log-likelihood function is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = S\\ln\\theta + (n-S)\\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nis the number of successes in the sample.\n\nTo compute a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimate, we differentiate $l(\\theta)$, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dl}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(S\\ln θ + (n - S)\\ln (1 - θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S}{θ} + \\frac{n - S}{1 - θ} \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S}{θ} + \\frac{n - S}{θ - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{S}{θ} + \\frac{n - S}{θ - 1} &= 0 \\\\\n(S(θ - 1) + (n - S)θ)/(θ(θ - 1)) &= 0 \\\\\nS(θ - 1) + (n - S)θ &= 0 \\\\\nSθ - S + nθ - Sθ &= 0 \\\\\nnθ - S &= 0 \\\\\nnθ &= S \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{S}{n} \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nwhich is simply the *mean of the sample*.\n\nThe maximum likelihood estimate for other common distributions can be derived similarly. Let's summarize the most important cases." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186163361 1064 "Let's derive a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimator for the unknown parameter $\\theta$ for a set of $n$ independent and identically distributed Bernoulli random variables.\n\nSuppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{n}$ is an I.I.D random sample, where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{n},$ the log-likelihood function is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = S\\ln\\theta + (n-S)\\ln(1-\\theta),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i$ is the number of successes in the sample. \n\nTo compute a general expression for the maximum likelihood estimate, we differentiate $l(\\theta),$ set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta.$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{S}{\\theta }+\\frac{n-S}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{S}{\\theta }+\\frac{n-S}{\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{S}{\\theta }+\\frac{n-S}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{S(\\theta -1)+(n-S)\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ S(\\theta -1)+(n-S)\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\cancel{S\\theta }-S+n\\theta -\\cancel{S\\theta } & =0 \\\\ n\\theta -S & =0 \\\\ n\\theta  & =S \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{S}{n} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta$ is \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,} =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i,\n$$\n\nwhich is simply the *mean of the sample*. \n\nThe maximum likelihood estimate for other common distributions can be derived similarly. Let's summarize the most important cases." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186163362 1064 "The table below summarizes the maximum likelihood estimates for several common probability distributions. These results can be derived by maximizing the corresponding log-likelihood function.\n\n| **Distribution** | **Probability Mass Function, $f(x; \\theta)$** | **Parameter, $\\theta$** | **Maximum Likelihood Estimate, $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Bernoulli | $\\displaystyle \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample |\n| Binomial | $\\displaystyle {N \\choose x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{N-x}$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample |\n| Poisson | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample |\n| Geometric | $\\displaystyle (1-\\theta)^{x-1} \\theta$ | The probability of success | $\\displaystyle \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$<br>The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Exponential | $\\displaystyle \\theta e^{-\\theta x}$ | The rate parameter | $\\displaystyle \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\bigg)^{-1}$<br>The reciprocal of the sample mean |\n| Normal | $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{\\theta_2 \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\frac{x-\\theta_1}{\\theta_2} \\right)^2}$ | The mean $(\\theta_1)$<br><br>The standard deviation $(\\theta_2)$ | $\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_1 = \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$<br>The mean of the sample<br><br>$\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}_2 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2}$<br>The standard deviation of the sample |" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186163362 1064 "The table below summarizes the maximum likelihood estimates for several common probability distributions. These results can be derived by maximizing the corresponding log-likelihood function. \n\n| **Distribution** | **Probability Mass Function, $f(x; \\theta)$** | **Parameter, $\\theta$** | **Maximum Likelihood Estimate, $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Bernoulli | $\\displaystyle \\theta^x(1-\\theta)