\\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1 - e^{-y})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}e^{-y}) \\\\\n&= e^{-y} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) \\\\\n&= e^{-y} \\cdot 2x \\\\\n&= 2xe^{-y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise,\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{2xe^{-y},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, y \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188005 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x \\partial y} F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $y \\geq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}(1-{e}^{-y}))) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{e}^{-y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y) = 0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n![](images/ce/cedf8a240fb8e0642642d5f39a4b174219e9b7c0283a5a3a469f431815e9909b.svg)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188065 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3$, $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 0.24$, and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0.2$. Determine $\\textrm{Var}[X-Y]$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188065 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3,$ $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 0.24,$ and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0.2.$ Determine $\\textrm{Var}[X-Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188065 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X \\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X - Y] = 3 + 0.24 - 2 \\cdot 0.2 = 2.84\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188065 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X \\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X - Y] = 3 + 0.24 - 2 \\cdot 0.2 = 2.84.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188066 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$, for $(x,y) \\in [0,1] \\times [0,2]$, is given by\n\n$F(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy}{8}(2x^2+y)$.\n\nFind $F_X(x)$, the marginal CDF of $X$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188066 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y) \\in [0,1] \\times [0,2],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy}{8}(2x^2+y).\n$$\n\nFind $F_X(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188066 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_X(x) = F(x,\\infty) = \\lim\\limits_{y \\to \\infty} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x\\leq 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{X}(x) &= F(x, ∞) \\\\\n&= F(x, 2) \\\\\n&= (x(2))/(8)(2x^{2} + (2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4x}{8}(x^{2} + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(x^{3} + x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188066 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_X(x) = F(x,\\infty) = \\lim\\limits_{y \\to \\infty} F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\leq x\\leq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & =F(x,\\infty ) \\\\  & =F(x,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2)}{8}(2{x}^{2}+(2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{8}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}+x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188067 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 5$, $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 2$, and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 3$. Find $\\textrm{Var}[2X - 5Y]$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188067 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 5,$ $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 2,$ and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 3.$ Find $\\textrm{Var}[2X - 5Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188067 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[2X - 5Y] &= 2^{2} \\cdot 5 + (-5)^{2} \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-5) \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 20 + 50 - 60 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188067 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X-5Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 5+(-5)^{2}\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =20+50-60 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188068 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X+2Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188068 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X+2Y]$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188068 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$. These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X]$. If $X$ is defined on a sample space $S_X$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.4 + 3 \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 1.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x^{2} \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 0^{2} \\cdot 0.4 + 3^{2} \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 5.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= 5.4 - 1.8^{2} \\\\\n&= 2.16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space $S_Y$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{y})y \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.5 + 2 \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y^{2}] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{y})y^{2} \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 0^{2} \\cdot 0.5 + 2^{2} \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= E[Y^{2}] - E[Y]^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$. We find $\\textrm{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)xy \\cdot f(x, y) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0 \\cdot f(0, 0) + 0 \\cdot 2 \\cdot f(0, 2) + 3 \\cdot 0 \\cdot f(3, 0) + 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot f(3, 2) \\\\\n&= 0 + 0 + 0 + 6 \\cdot 0.2 \\\\\n&= 1.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[XY] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 1.2 - 1.8 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-0.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[5X + 2Y] &= 5^{2} \\cdot 2.16 + 2^{2} \\c