\\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\textrm{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot f(0,0)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+3\\cdot 0\\cdot f(3,0)+3\\cdot 2\\cdot f(3,2) \\\\  & =0+0+0+6\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.2-1.8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X+2Y] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot 2.16+{2}^{2}\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 5\\cdot 2\\cdot (-0.6) \\\\  & =54+4-12 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188069 1071 "For three random variables $X_1,X_2$, and $X_3$, we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$\\textrm{Var}[X_1] = 1, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_2] = 3, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_3] = 8 \\qquad$\n\n$\\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2] = 2, \\qquad \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_3] = 4, \\qquad \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_3] = -5 \\qquad$\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X_1 + 2X_2 + 3X_3]$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188069 1071 "For three random variables $X_1,X_2,$ and $X_3,$ we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_1] = 1,   \\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[X_2] = 3,   \\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[X_3] = 8   \\qquad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2] = 2,   \\qquad\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_3] = 4,   \\qquad\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_3] = -5   \\qquad\n$$\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X_1 + 2X_2 + 3X_3].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188069 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, and $a_1, \\ldots, a_n$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\left[\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i X_i \\right]\n= \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\textrm{Var}[X_i] + 2\\sum\\limits_{i <     j} a_i a_j \\textrm{Cov}[X_i,X_j].\n$$\n\nHence, for $n=3$ random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\