n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.8-{1.6}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We compute $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+1\\cdot 1\\cdot f(1,1)+1\\cdot 2\\cdot f(1,2) \\\\  & =0+0+1\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.3-0.8\\cdot 1.6 \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X+3Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.16+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.24+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot 0.02 \\\\  & =0.64+2.16+0.24 \\\\  & =3.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188845 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below.\n\n$F_{X}(x) = {1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwiseF_{Y}(y) = {1 - \\frac{1}{y},\\begin{vmatrix}y \\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x \\ge 1$ and $y \\ge 1$." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186188845 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{y}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186188845 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})(1-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186189381 1064 "The first property to consider is how to recover the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y$ from the joint CDF.\n\nSuppose the joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{(1 - e^{-x})(1 - e^{-2y}),\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, y \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $F_Y(y)$, the marginal CDF of $Y$ using the following property:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = F(\\infty,y) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for\n\n$$\ny \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{Y}(y) &= lim_(x → ∞)[(1 - e^{-x})(1 - e^{-2y})] \\\\\n&= (1 - e^{-2y})lim_(x → ∞)(1 - \\frac{1}{e^{x}}) \\\\\n&= (1 - e^{-2y})lim_(x → ∞)(1 - 0) \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for the marginal CDF is as follows:\n\n$$\n{1 - e^{-2y},\\begin{vmatrix}y \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186189381 1064 "The first property to consider is how to recover the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y$ from the joint CDF. \n\nSuppose the joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n![](images/ec/ecc23b4f4772364ee423406c3bb030b51129972d7c3c49cb03e35f8aca638485.svg)\n\nLet's calculate $F_Y(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y$ using the following property:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = F(\\infty,y) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $y \\geq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[(1-{e}^{-x})(1-{e}^{-2y})] \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y}){lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y}){lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for the marginal CDF is as follows:\n\n![](images/ae/ae315a3011daa44612e13dec66cc848b5b06ba4002a4baf183e5edabc13ad974.svg)\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186189413 1064 "Given two random variables $X$ and $Y$, the variance of the sum or difference of these variables is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance of $X$ and $Y$. We'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\nNote that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]= 0\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y]\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186189413 1064 "Given two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the variance of the sum or difference of these variables is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y],\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ We'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\nNote that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]= 0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y].\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186189414 1064 "If $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab \\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0\n$$\n\nand we recover the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y]\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186189414 1064 "If $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab \\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0,$ and we recover the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] .\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186189421 1064 "Let's prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X+ Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of variance and using the linearity of expectation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X + Y] &= E[((X + Y) - E[X + Y])^{2}] \\\\\n&= E[((X - E[X]) + (Y - E[Y]))^{2}] \\\\\n&= E[(X - E[X])^{2} + (Y - E[Y])^{2} + 2(X - E[X])(Y - E[Y])]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDistributing the expected value, and using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[aX] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X]\n$$\n\nfor constant $a$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X + Y] &= E[(X - E[X])^{2} + (Y - E[Y])^{2} + 2(X - E[X])(Y - E[Y])] \\\\\n&= E[(X - E[X])^{2}] + E[(Y - E[Y])^{2}] + 2 \\cdot E[(X - E[X])(Y - E[Y])] \\\\\n&= Var[X] + Var[Y] + 2Cov[X, Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThe proof for the difference between two random variables is similar." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186189421 1064 "Let's prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X+ Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of variance and using the linearity of expectation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[((X+Y)-\\text{E}[X+Y])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[((X-\\text{E}[X])+(Y-\\text{E}[Y]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}+(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}+2(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDistributing the expected value, and using the fact that $\\textrm{E}[aX] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X]$ for constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}+(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}+2(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}]+\\text{E}[(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}]+2\\cdot \\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]+2\\text{Cov}[X,Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nThe proof for the difference between two random variables is similar." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186189432 1064 "Recall that the **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X \\leq x,Y \\leq y)\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$, we have the following relationship between the joint PDF and joint CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\int^y_{-\\infty}\\int^x_{-\\infty} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u\\textrm{d}v\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the joint CDF gives the probability that the random pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region $\\Delta$ that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\n![](images/ee/ee82c6b810f02f8b8380bd97c94cbe491095d17dbadc685ffdca1a54cf9828b8.png)\n\nThe joint CDF satisfies the following properties:\n\n- $0\\leq F(x,y)\\leq 1$\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty)=1$\n- $F(-\\infty,y)=F(x,-\\infty)=0$\n- The **marginal CDF** of $X$ is given by $F_X(x)=F(x,\\infty)$.\n- The **marginal CDF** of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y)=F(\\infty,y)$.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then\n$F(x,y)=F_X(x)\\cdot F_Y(y)$.\n- The joint probability density function $f(x, y)$ is given by\n$f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x \\partial y} F(x,y)$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll explore the last four of these properties in more detail." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186189432 1064 "Recall that the **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X \\leq x,Y \\leq y).\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$, we have the following relationship between the joint PDF and joint CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\int^y_{-\\infty}\\int^x_{-\\infty} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u\\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the joint CDF gives the probability that the random pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region $\\Delta$ that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\n![](images/ee/ee82c6b810f02f8b8380bd97c94cbe491095d17dbadc685ffdca1a54cf9828b8.png)\n\nThe joint CDF satisfies the following properties:\n\n- $0\\leq F(x,y)\\leq 1$\n\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty)=1$\n\n- $F(-\\infty ,y)=F(x,-\\infty)=0$\n\n- The **marginal CDF** of $X$ is given by $F_X(x)=F(x,\\infty).$\n\n- The **marginal CDF** of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y)=F(\\infty,y).$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then \n$F(x,y)=F_X(x)\\cdot F_Y(y).$\n\n- The joint probability density function $f(x, y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x \\partial y} F(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll explore the last four of these properties in more detail." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186198370 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527136 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186247211 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186247211 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186247211 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\},$ and the event $A=\\{X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/ea/ea585fb6e12672f5cc854578cb12ff0357cfcd8d2f3d3b75a82723241cdaf3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=x}^{y=\\pi /2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.793.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186247211 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},$ and the event $A={X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/ea/ea585fb6e12672f5cc854578cb12ff0357cfcd8d2f3d3b75a82723241cdaf3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=x}^{y=\\pi /2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.793.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186247214 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186247214 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186247214 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X>1\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/26/26cbd44827c3a5a3fde5d61b631ce4f214bcf9a36ce6bf76129b1fe9f9478eff.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186247214 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A={X>1}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/26/26cbd44827c3a5a3fde5d61b631ce4f214bcf9a36ce6bf76129b1fe9f9478eff.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186396318 1071 "Consider a function $b(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{b(t)\\}=B(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186396318 1071 "Consider a function $b(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{b(t)\\}=B(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186398726 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by two units to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-2))=u(2-t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(2-t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(2-t).$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186398726 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by two units to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-2))=u(2-t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(2-t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(2-t).$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186411821 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526921 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186411821 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411689 13194139535762 17592186045417 false] [17592186411821 1078 #edb/ref 17592186526921 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186526486 1043 #edb/ref 17592186527235 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186526486 1043 #edb/ref 17592186527240 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186526486 1043 #edb/ref 17592186527256 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186526486 1043 #edb/ref 17592186527258 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186526486 1043 #edb/ref 17592186527262 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186526486 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [1759218