 [17592186527121 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526705 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527121 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527006 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527121 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527121 1077 "selection" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527121 1078 #edb/ref 17592186526584 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527121 1079 #uuid "fdf514b2-8d3a-5a58-a6b9-5e62c2d37fa5" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527030 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527122 1069 "q-3599" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527122 1071 "Factor ${u}^{3}+{v}^{3}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527122 1072 #uuid "0184b3b4-f2c2-5b99-95bf-75c75b254d85" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527122 1074 "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}+{b}^{3}=(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nWe factor our expression using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}+{v}^{3} & =(u+v)({u}^{2}-uv+{v}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527048 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527123 1069 "q-185123" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527123 1071 "$(-70)^{2}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527123 1072 #uuid "01e96138-a1bd-5aa7-aa98-7c5862390efd" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527123 1074 "To work out $(-70)^{2},$ we multiply $-70$ by itself:\n\n$$\n(-70)^{2}=(-70)\\cdot (-70)=4,900\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527013 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527124 1069 "q-159335" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527124 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{xy-y}{x}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & x\\ge 1,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527124 1072 #uuid "02209bdf-5c25-5d71-9f0c-90055349806b" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527124 1074 "The marginal CDF of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{xy-y}{x}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{y(x-1)}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =y{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527065 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527125 1069 "q-185189" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527125 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527125 1072 #uuid "056f7c20-573d-55c9-bb54-7bee0a1479fd" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527125 1074 "To work out ${(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2},$ we multiply $\\frac{5}{3}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{25}{9}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527067 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527126 1069 "q-148325" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527126 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=2,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=4,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=3.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X+2Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527126 1072 #uuid "079f02ad-9ee0-58ac-bead-d4394104b125" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527126 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+2Y] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 2+{2}^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2+16+12 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527044 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527127 1069 "q-123618" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527127 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $\\frac{13\\pi }{9}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527127 1072 #uuid "093fd520-21d8-5c0d-89e8-fac985077f71" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527127 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{13\\pi }{9})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={260}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\frac{13\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to ${260}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/50/50db86bd273e9e20ae705890103b067a1f664ed912fcb6efe9083db1060625c8.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527063 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527128 1069 "q-60606" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527128 1071 "![](images/84/84da1d1bb056c8c5d823a585889699e3ba3fe5ca7b2706b4e0a16c96f2e65ac3.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(3,1)$  and $(3,4)?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527128 1072 #uuid "0a27d18e-df05-520d-948c-41b1732464a2" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527128 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3.$\n\n![](images/a0/a0eb36fd37d73ed20e0ee17717f80cb33a56910cc57eb181897ad44197a7f268.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/2b/2b4771270aec2dd62828f4063262cfaf81fba31f6be6d1a2ba8bfe0d59778b48.png)\n\nThe point $(3,4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(3,1)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4-1=3.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527082 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527129 1069 "q-67258" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527129 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $1$st term is $0$ and the $9$th term is $40.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527129 1072 #uuid "0a8909ee-fe18-5d80-9c79-9f98ec446962" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527129 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is ${a}_{1}=0,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$ \n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{1}=0$ and ${a}_{9}=40$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{1}}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-0}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{8} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =0+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =5n-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527051 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527130 1069 "q-5430" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527130 1071 "Find the general formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $7$th term is $-18$ and the first term is $6.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527130 1072 #uuid "0bf79e97-49c2-5006-b045-7fdc65e739fd" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527130 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is ${a}_{1}=6,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$ \n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{1}=6$ and ${a}_{7}=-18$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{7}-{a}_{1}}{7-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-18-6}{7-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24}{6} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  the formula for the $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =6+(n-1)(-4) \\\\  & =6-4n+4 \\\\  & =10-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527113 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527131 1069 "q-317323" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527131 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527131 1072 #uuid "0fb80f93-0987-58b5-ba12-42ac451dfbe6" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527131 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/01/0183b9256896263aadfb0d18ae0a88aa11152c6e7e7d5693771f2e5bac20ac0a.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/31/31c5930bbeca76437d8e7a642bf52da4814bc5afda681b7974b383d3b8762c21.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $6$ units up to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+6.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=6.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4092a81a26d34360480e2ee46e99a17fa740b398038f36de427f3ed839bc68b.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527087 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527132 1069 "q-18347" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527132 1071 "![](images/40/4030ed55caa6900e6927021a1b4819d4fd2157b803fc23ccc05165ba01bd3fce.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527132 1072 #uuid "128afa31-6089-55bb-b4c5-d34347e7a11c" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527132 1074 "This is the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ that's been translated $3$ units *left*.\n\n![](images/d9/d933bb153126e39091c73df703facc27e149fb793021eb4b38a5456b76d42bb1.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=-(x\\,+\\,3)^{3}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527022 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527133 1069 "q-177698" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527133 1071 "The $7$th and $8$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $-13$ and $-20,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527133 1072 #uuid "1617bc78-d7dd-5411-87bd-2e3b8377e377" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527133 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{7}=-13$ and ${a}_{8}=-20$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{8}-{a}_{7} \\\\  & =-20-(-13) \\\\  & =-20+13 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{8}=-20,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{8}$ is $7$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & ={a}_{1}+7d \\\\ -20 & ={a}_{1}+7(-7) \\\\ -20 & ={a}_{1}-49 \\\\ -20+49 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =29+(n-1)(-7) \\\\  & =29-7n+7 \\\\  & =36-7n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527086 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527134 1069 "q-148330" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527134 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=0.24,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=0.06,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.08.$ Find $\\text{Var}[2X-8Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527134 1072 #uuid "1e6dcf9d-d6cd-51ac-b73d-58cf9701a199" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527134 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X-8Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.24+(-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.06+2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-8)\\cdot 0.08 \\\\  & =0.96+3.84-2.56 \\\\  & =2.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527100 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527135 1069 "q-89848" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527135 1071 "![](images/49/496cfd2dc0864e509a1f3f958f2578cbe70684a19b93aecf89d6b38b8c7f5680.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527135 1072 #uuid "1fd4a51b-43a7-5d87-a486-e59b745ec11b" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527135 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph III has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527040 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527136 1069 "q-160572" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527136 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527136 1072 #uuid "216ab53f-9c7f-5387-ad09-67452d907209" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527136 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x}{y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ \\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y\\cdot (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})-x\\cdot \\frac{2x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3v}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527039 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527137 1069 "q-169408" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527137 1071 "Anna makes two cakes. The first cake weighs $1,200\\,\\text{g},$ while the second cake weighs $140%$ of the weight of the first. What is the weight of the second cake?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527137 1072 #uuid "21a2d2fe-461f-5c6a-9efc-076472c2d6af" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527137 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the first cake, which is $1,200\\,\\text{g}.$ The percentage is $140%,$ and the part is the weight of the second cake.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n140%=140÷100=1.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =140%\\times 1,200 \\\\  & =1.4\\times 1,200 \\\\  & =1,680\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $140%$ of $1,200$ is $1,680.$ So, the weight of the second cake is $1,680\\,\\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n140%=\\frac{140}{100}=\\frac{140÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =140%\\times 1,200 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}\\times 1,200 \\\\  & =7\\times \\frac{1,200}{5} \\\\  & =7\\times 240 \\\\  & =1,680\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $140%$ of $1,200$ is $1,680.$ So, the weight of the second cake is $1,680\\,\\text{g}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527062 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527138 1069 "q-160597" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527138 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(0,2)$  and $(0,5)?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527138 1072 #uuid "23bd23d2-9b32-5ac7-a70a-faac16c61773" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527138 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/f3/f329376e27ec08d2695db0e6d007e7a7b5342e7737c5d3f45eb524a2b1c2e572.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3.$\n\n![](images/66/66543bc73667ed70a965db4af2e657ad991660fc1b3a73a6e585943deffd3bbb.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/5d/5ded01b570e62d1e206aa8abd1012121367e60e749d441eb4e6ec7e3b12f6e8a.png)\n\nThe point $(0,5)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(0,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5-2=3.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527015 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527139 1069 "q-37554" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527139 1071 "Factor the expression ${q}^{6}-27{d}^{3}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527139 1072 #uuid "246f9007-c107-5fa6-a212-74a6a9a70dc6" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527139 1074 "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\n{q}^{6}-27{d}^{3}={({q}^{2})}^{3}-{(3d)}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the given expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{6}-27{d}^{3} & ={({q}^{2})}^{3}-{(3d)}^{3} \\\\  & =({q}^{2}-3d)(({q}^{2})^{2}+{q}^{2}\\cdot 3d+(3d)^{2}) \\\\  & =({q}^{2}-3d)({q}^{4}+3d{q}^{2}+9{d}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527021 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527140 1069 "q-159330" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527140 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>1 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527140 1072 #uuid "2603d8b7-b055-59f9-a9de-a5bcf92cb12b" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527140 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527016 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527141 1069 "q-18349" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527141 1071 "![](images/46/46d382b4d31818e63f917b8df074a5710e187362fc1aaa125a28245a371564cd.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527141 1072 #uuid "26a8d25b-e6dd-5460-9414-80558f8bec9d" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527141 1074 "This is the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ that's been translated \n\n- $2$ units to the *right*, and\n\n- $1$ unit *up*.\n\n![](images/4d/4d0840ff427022b8bf23ebd3d36d58bf0e5bbe75d339f139ed86930d53eec09d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=-(x\\,-\\,2)^{3}\\,+\\,1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527041 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527142 1069 "q-177715" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527142 1071 "Find the $14$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $3$rd term is $40$ and the $23$rd term is $-20.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527142 1072 #uuid "280035db-18a9-57e4-8578-03884e2e2b26" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527142 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\n \nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{3}=40$ and ${a}_{23}=-20$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{23}-{a}_{3}}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20-40}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-60)}{20} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{3}=40,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}+2(-3) \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}-6 \\\\ 40+6 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =46+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =46-3n+3 \\\\  & =49-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $14$th term by substituting $n=14$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{14} & =49-3(14) \\\\  & =49-42 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527121 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527143 1069 "q-130720" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527143 1071 "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{3}{y}^{2}=2x+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,1).$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527143 1072 #uuid "28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527143 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{y}^{2} & =2x+y \\\\ {x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2)\\,i+(2{x}^{3}y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =(3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(2(-1)^{3}(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,1)=⟨1,-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x+1) & =y-1 \\\\ -3x-3 & =y-1 \\\\ 3x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527042 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527144 1069 "q-147969" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527144 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527144 1072 #uuid "29a09d79-668a-5d76-93b7-d90b94b6b6a1" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527144 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.85$ | $0.15$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0+1.2 \\\\  & =1.2 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.85+1\\cdot 0.15 \\\\  & =0+0.15 \\\\  & =0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.2)(y-0.15)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0-1.2)(0-0.15)f(0,0) \\\\  & +(0-1.2)(1-0.15)f(0,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(0-0.15)f(2,0) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(1-0.15)f(2,1) \\\\  & =(-1.2)(-0.15)(0.25) \\\\  & +(-1.2)(0.85)(0.15) \\\\  & +(0.8)(-0.15)(0.6) \\\\  & +(0.8)(0.85)(0) \\\\  & =-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527109 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527145 1069 "q-18340" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527145 1071 "![](images/74/74718361ade9a3a1a5123cbebe26baf2b49a6ae4739b544fe5b41578bd71d038.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527145 1072 #uuid "2b8be0e4-6ba2-573c-9c5f-c0fd730c101c" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527145 1074 "This is the curve $y={x}^{3}$ that's been translated \n\n- $2$ units to the *right*, and\n\n- $2$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/c6/c652e1639e68ecc5f09fccce36ed236e7bad3f99d704e95c40286afa5db66963.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=(x\\,-\\,2)^{3}\\,-\\,2.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527080 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527146 1069 "q-143507" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527146 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{75}$ be a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75,$ calculate $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527146 1072 #uuid "2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527146 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =75,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{0.75}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.01).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =P(\\frac{1.5-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}<Z<\\frac{1.6-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}) \\\\  & =P(0<Z<1) \\\\  & =P(Z<1)-P(Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(1)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution, $Φ(0)=0.5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =Φ(1)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =0.8413-0.5 \\\\  & =0.3413.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527028 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527147 1069 "q-159941" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527147 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-8.4+7.4\\text{ }\\,\\theta -6\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527147 1072 #uuid "31869bf1-d8be-5a63-a737-e3c2335ce819" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527147 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-8.4+7.4\\text{ }\\,\\theta -6\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =7.4-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.4-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =7.4 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.617\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.617,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527074 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527148 1069 "q-102691" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527148 1071 "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1).$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527148 1072 #uuid "33021c77-54f7-572b-82bd-06940f46518d" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527148 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}-2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=2{y}^{2}-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2})=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2})=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=0\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-16.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527096 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527149 1069 "q-85461" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527149 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $11\\,289\\,643$\n\nII. $13\\,298\\,784$\n\nIII. $215\\,153\\,238$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527149 1072 #uuid "33724016-d9da-53c5-bcf3-d48acaf1fa22" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527149 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$\n\n- The number $11\\,289\\,643$ is not divisible by $2,$ so it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- The number $13\\,298\\,784$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n1+3+2+9+8+7+8+4=42\n$$\n\nSo, $13\\,298\\,784$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $215\\,153\\,238$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n2+1+5+1+5+3+2+3+8=30\n$$\n\nSo, $215\\,153\\,238$ is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527019 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527150 1069 "q-155548" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527150 1071 "![](images/86/867e0d53894e1cacac86120e5c79b32071e2b0a4863b445e6d3492392a59461d.png)\n\nConsider the figure shown above. Which of the following triplets of points are collinear?\n\nI. $M,Q,J$\n\nII. $P,O,L$\n\nIII. $L,O,M$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527150 1072 #uuid "34603e37-b60f-5eb1-996c-5382f05619c6" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527150 1074 "- $M,Q,J$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/7f/7f707d8fbeb9853ccb9441bbdab3b0674c5f51da7f73da73d73ee6592dca04ea.png)\n\n- $P,O,L$ are **not** collinear since we can't draw a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/02/028ae56c8a85699d4d6719df2f80a989f1271e392546d005093319af2365675d.png)\n\n \n\n- $L,O,M$ are collinear since there is a straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/bf/bf0579ecbc33ebe2d97dc1b305de0a89b0c22dadd6a11d0e88ac4a36acbd447f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527090 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527151 1069 "q-3529" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527151 1071 "Factor $27{c}^{3}+1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527151 1072 #uuid "36ce6c6d-22d9-5124-bf0e-5ddb030e27ef" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527151 1074 "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}+{b}^{3}=(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a sum of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n27{c}^{3}+1=(3c)^{3}+{1}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27{c}^{3}+1 & =(3c)^{3}+{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(3c+1)((3c)^{2}-3c\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3c+1)(9{c}^{2}-3c+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527049 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527152 1069 "q-159320" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527152 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{1-y}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527152 1072 #uuid "3a66a770-559e-57c4-8545-74993f2211db" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527152 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})(1-{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-{e}^{1-y}+\\frac{{e}^{1-y}}{\\sqrt{x}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527091 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527153 1069 "q-61265" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527153 1071 "What is the distance between the points $(-3,2)$  and $(-3,-4)?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527153 1072 #uuid "3ab19b4a-4627-54ee-8e9e-8d051f6ac141" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527153 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/c0/c0dcef8b6c0c63e17fc9b516e3e3176f054dd0eddb93c71375a3de3272da9623.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1764499f4600d5c74c598b205e0a1971ca91231c4464f77e0809a680003bf11.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/5b/5b4c4c665b07575604dc873703517548404d7c31e8395070642ca37584c6d08f.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,-4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4+2=6.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527107 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527154 1069 "q-185181" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527154 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527154 1072 #uuid "3cfc1cc8-75ac-58cb-baba-115d9b2db8af" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527154 1074 "To work out ${(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{8}{5}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} & = \\\\ (-\\frac{8}{5})\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{5}) & = \\\\ \\frac{8\\cdot 8}{5\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{64}{25} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527105 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527155 1069 "q-28208" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527155 1071 "![](images/7d/7d341093cf35425a75174a6871d452c38daa0d281cdc2a2b7aaceeca390dee5b.png)\n\nWhich of the following curves is the graph of $y={x}^{3}?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527155 1072 #uuid "3f99401c-4f2a-57bb-bccb-f67190e5a3b3" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527155 1074 "First, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y={x}^{3}$ increases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ The only graphs that exhibit this increasing property are II and IV.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs II and IV. To do this, let's evaluate $y={x}^{3}$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=-1.$ Substituting $x=-1$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny=(-1)^{3}=-1,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(-1,-1).$ Only graph IV satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph IV corresponds to the graph of $y={x}^{3}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527032 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527156 1069 "q-148346" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527156 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X-Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527156 1072 #uuid "43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527156 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =1.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =2.8-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.5+1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =0.5-{0.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot f(0,0)+0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+2\\cdot 0\\cdot f(2,0)+2\\cdot 1\\cdot f(2,1) \\\\  & =0+0+0+2\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =0.6-1.4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 0.84+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-0.1) \\\\  & =7.56+0.25+0.6 \\\\  & =8.41.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527018 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527157 1069 "q-159349" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527157 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={log}_{2}⁡(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527157 1072 #uuid "431dc348-674b-5da2-8e50-c9106a497552" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527157 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+(1){y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(1+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527110 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527158 1069 "q-159327" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527158 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>4 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527158 1072 #uuid "433f0ce6-f1e1-52cc-bff5-350654ecc1ba" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527158 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{48}xy(x+y))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+x\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+2y\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}(2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527038 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527159 1069 "q-159347" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527159 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)).\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527159 1072 #uuid "449c39ae-346f-5e58-9576-63532dc73237" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527159 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(\\frac{\\pi }{2},y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527033 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527160 1069 "q-159939" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527160 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent exponential random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527160 1072 #uuid "4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527160 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\theta } & =15.8 \\\\ 5 & =15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.316\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.316,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527097 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527161 1069 "q-75596" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527161 1071 "What is the constant term in the expression $\\frac{2}{3}+6a-4b?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527161 1072 #uuid "454efe3b-e281-5051-bb7a-cf90c5710624" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527161 1074 "The expression has three terms: $\\frac{2}{3}$, $6a$, and $-4b.$  The only term that does not contain any variables is $\\frac{2}{3}$, so $\\frac{2}{3}$ is the constant term." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527095 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527162 1069 "q-111955" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527162 1071 "![](images/05/05173f7d9b4cdf89d4167f71533da6b95942bf6355bc6245fc95fbd7c434440e.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above show the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527162 1072 #uuid "4aa71abb-1f45-5c68-95b9-e97d2bf13015" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527162 1074 "First, notice that graph III resembles a straight line. So, we disregard this option.\n\nNow, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}$ decreases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ The only graphs that exhibit this decreasing property are I and II.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs I and II. To do this, let's evaluate $y=-{x}^{3}$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}=0,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(0,0).$ Only graph II satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph II corresponds to the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527115 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527163 1069 "q-71400" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527163 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527163 1072 #uuid "4bf356ec-77aa-55f5-9268-fd51c57a8d9c" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527163 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j+\\frac{1}{1}\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527084 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527164 1069 "q-6647" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527164 1071 "![](images/0f/0f17474f6837ba685a64fa7681df485c2495f38ed789355361b4117b814b9dbc.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527164 1072 #uuid "51849fa8-ba8b-5524-9925-28a75279e347" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527164 1074 "This is the curve $y={x}^{3}$ that's been translated $2$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/60/60daebed5585a53e1e3154ba568bd4baa4b223cb6cb7fe95ebffda291e23e9a6.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y={x}^{3}\\,-\\,2.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527059 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527165 1069 "q-64489" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527165 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of ${135}^{∘}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527165 1072 #uuid "519a97bb-018e-5643-989a-45e979fef136" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527165 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and a vertex that lies on the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/e3/e3e61526a1d903bc577d6e2d255f775567ad026a778f20cb8892ee8611c11a02.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527017 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527166 1069 "q-159945" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527166 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-9.6+12.6\\text{ }\\,\\theta -8\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527166 1072 #uuid "54d12174-c052-5371-9f75-2200e071a1fb" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527166 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-9.6+12.6\\text{ }\\,\\theta -8\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =12.6-16\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.6-16\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 16\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =12.6 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.788\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.788,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527026 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527167 1069 "q-181162" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527167 1071 "The value of $12÷(-5),$ expressed as an **improper fraction** in lowest terms, is{{field-1}}." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527167 1072 #uuid "61f14a3a-d997-54ee-80e7-e8241a88c524" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527167 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $(12)$ by a negative one $(-5).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n12÷(-5)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $12$ by $5$ without any signs: $12÷5=\\frac{12}{5}.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n12÷(-5)=-\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527070 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527168 1069 "q-61105" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527168 1071 "![](images/25/25e1891ce31fa7043723b2dcb4cd1e01c41dbd82ae70ef5024c64ed2d6deab33.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(-3,1)$  and $(-3,-5)?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527168 1072 #uuid "67699b33-3e46-5c7c-ad2b-2f6df6bd8eb1" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527168 1074 "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](images/1f/1f3eeede9aa301cf6d64b48cc86f770fc86517ed51285da9bdc86e5a580b243f.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/a5/a504ca22769a377cd7e23e0b6a09595d00c8ad25733f3d1e6a716b52f262e139.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,-5)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,1)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5+1=6.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527046 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527169 1069 "q-119548" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527169 1071 "Find the value of $\\frac{2}{3}-1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527169 1072 #uuid "72a5b03b-3213-5354-ab4d-cb82e383745f" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527169 1074 "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nSince both fractions have the same denominators, we can combine them into one fraction, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{2-4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $2-4$ using a number line:\n\n![](images/fd/fd7fc3fedd2dbf8c90592bbedfdc15181eae63e4d2cbd71a4c036b2d75e8921c.png)\n\nSo, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{2-4}{3}=\\frac{-2}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527024 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527170 1069 "q-159311" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527170 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527170 1072 #uuid "74fc0efc-8b80-569c-9c95-98f9405e5eca" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527170 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-2)({y}^{2}-1)}{x{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-2}{x}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527094 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527171 1069 "q-148013" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527171 1071 "If $\\text{E}[X]=5.5,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=4.38,$ and $\\text{E}[XY]=24.12$ for two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527171 1072 #uuid "7980335d-9e66-542b-a520-d910d32ed247" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527171 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =24.12-(5.5)\\cdot (4.38) \\\\  & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of $X$ and $Y$ is $0.03.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527093 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527172 1069 "q-138973" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527172 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527172 1072 #uuid "7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527172 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527083 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527173 1069 "q-148351" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527173 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X+4Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527173 1072 #uuid "7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527173 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.55$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.45+4\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =2.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.45+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =8.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =8.8-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =3.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.5-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+4\\cdot 1\\cdot f(4,1)+4\\cdot 2\\cdot f(4,2) \\\\  & =0+0+4\\cdot 0.2+8\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =3.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3.6-2.2\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X+4Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 3.96+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =35.64+4+7.2 \\\\  & =46.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527112 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527174 1069 "q-69733" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527174 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-(x-3)^{3}?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527174 1072 #uuid "818b8208-c98b-5ec3-a330-6bbba9ff0a1a" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527174 1074 "Since we have $-3$ inside the brackets, we translate the graph of $y=-{x}^{3}$ by $3$ units to the *right*. \n\nSo, the graph of $y=-(x-3)^{3}$ looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e4/e4afdd5a6c2b466b9447f7c9c0d0e4c885f17aa219e557bf9589bff88aeea0ae.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527101 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527175 1069 "q-159344" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527175 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1],$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{Y}(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y,$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527175 1072 #uuid "856af2a4-8ad4-5acc-a980-a089f44a32f5" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527175 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=F(\\infty ,y)={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =F(\\infty ,y) \\\\  & =F(1,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1){y}^{2}+(1)^{2}y}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527098 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527176 1069 "q-117935" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527176 1071 "![](images/3e/3e1675e22ef33aa40b96349c910f04178dd4b11b1e7e97f15a5314977eea914f.png)\n\nWhich vertices are adjacent to $F?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527176 1072 #uuid "8670832a-c9ac-5ad5-acea-ff50047338a5" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527176 1074 "$F$ is the endpoint of the sides $\\overset{―}{FG}$ and $\\overset{―}{EF}.$ Therefore, the vertices $E$ and $G$ are adjacent to $F.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527060 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527177 1069 "q-159337" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527177 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,y\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{X}(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $0\\le x\\le 2.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527177 1072 #uuid "86ba6c5d-d640-5e57-88b7-9b3f13232b7b" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527177 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=F(x,\\infty )={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }F(x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={lim}_{y\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y}{2\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{y\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527102 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527178 1069 "q-111250" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527178 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>Y).$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527178 1072 #uuid "86df2b6f-b298-58e2-a359-d3e3b918ef94" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527178 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X>Y\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/2d/2db85950b4d45901d79c173f264173d0c38046b2f087d0a28c8d5c69f024e31f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>Y) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527056 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527179 1069 "q-75520" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527179 1071 "${7.3}^{2}=$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527179 1072 #uuid "8c77c325-2e41-51df-821c-943abffd054d" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527179 1074 "We need to calculate the product of $7.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{7.3}^{2}=(7.3)\\cdot (7.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $73\\times 73=5\\,329.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($7.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($7.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $5\\,329$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n53\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{29}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${7.3}^{2}=53.29\\,.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527027 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527180 1069 "q-159963" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527180 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ),\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527180 1072 #uuid "8cb508aa-a9ee-56b2-be2a-f7d71a96d61c" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527180 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\theta }+\\frac{12}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\theta }+\\frac{12}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{\\theta }+\\frac{12}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8(\\theta -1)+12\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 8(\\theta -1)+12\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 20\\theta -8 & =0 \\\\ 20\\theta  & =8 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{2}{5}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527117 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527181 1069 "q-89855" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527181 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. The range of $f(x)$ is $(-10,\\infty )$\n\nII. $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\nIII. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the second and fourth quadrants" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527181 1072 #uuid "90b71102-b1fe-54a8-9326-1ad18a9d698b" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527181 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since the range of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527072 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527182 1069 "q-185205" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527182 1071 "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(-4.5)^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527182 1072 #uuid "9362856b-6dfd-5551-b35f-afef6a56607c" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527182 1074 "We need to calculate the product of $(-4.5)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-4.5)^{2}=(-4.5)\\cdot (-4.5)=(4.5)\\cdot (4.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45\\times 45=2\\,025.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($4.5$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($4.5$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,025$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n20\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{25}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-4.5)^{2}=20.25\\,.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527036 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527183 1069 "q-148315" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527183 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{7}{4},$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X-Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527183 1072 #uuid "94546e94-1af9-54e7-9a1b-29fb0570cfc8" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527183 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X\\pm Y]=\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]\\pm 2\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X-Y]=\\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{5}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527031 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527184 1069 "q-159970" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527184 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Poisson random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527184 1072 #uuid "953449db-a540-520d-adff-108b01b9d257" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527184 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24) \\\\  & =-4+\\frac{8}{\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+\\frac{8}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-4\\theta +8}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ -4\\theta +8 & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=2.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527058 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527185 1069 "q-102967" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527185 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty $\n\nII. The range of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,0]$\n\nIII. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527185 1072 #uuid "98e4ebcc-8114-515c-b8a9-5feafd8f7bb3" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527185 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false since the range of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527075 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527186 1069 "q-143871" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527186 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{(X+Y)}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & y\\le x\\le 4-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527186 1072 #uuid "9a19ca23-6c60-5d6e-8799-38d55529cc16" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527186 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/8d/8d1935944ffa0017da736fc540ff823df8a052c2c32c985553cde08ea5fce8da.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{(X+Y)}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}(x+y)^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}(x+y)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}(x+y)^{3}]}_{x=y}^{x=4-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{1}{3}(4-y+y)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(y+y)^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({4}^{3}-(2y)^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(64-8{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[64y-2{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(128-32) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527089 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527187 1069 "q-143884" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527187 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{e}^{X-Y}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527187 1072 #uuid "9ba35bb1-196e-51b8-b71e-102fe895702c" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527187 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{e}^{X-Y}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{e}^{x-y}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{e}^{x}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(e-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[(e-1)y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =(e-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527014 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527037 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527188 1069 "q-171509" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527188 1071 "$8(2u-5v)=${{field-1}}\n$u\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$v$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527188 1072 #uuid "9d8b1706-5dbe-56db-8638-95f308211cb0" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527188 1074 "Using the distributive law, we multiply each term inside the parentheses by the number $8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(2u-5v) & = \\\\ 8\\cdot 2u+8\\cdot (-5v) & = \\\\ (16u)+(-40v) & = \\\\ 16u-40v\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527078 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527189 1069 "q-148310" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527189 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=4,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=1,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=1.2.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X-Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527189 1072 #uuid "a0026095-8676-593f-8d69-3fa08a38ef1b" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527189 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X\\pm Y]=\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]\\pm 2\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X-Y]=4+1-2\\cdot 1.2=2.6.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527054 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527190 1069 "q-159322" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527190 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-2y}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527190 1072 #uuid "a27194f1-04ae-54c8-8fbb-2f24bc3ed222" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527190 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{2+x})(1-{e}^{-2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2+x}(1-{e}^{-2y}), & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527034 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527191 1069 "q-92945" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527191 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing numbers in the following sentence?\n\nTo paint his car, Anthony mixes blue paint with thinner. ​He requires $\\frac{3}{4}$ gallons of paint for every gallon of thinner. Therefore, the mixture has a ratio of $\\boxed{}$ gallons of paint to $\\boxed{}$ gallons of thinner." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527191 1072 #uuid "a55cec5a-6ff7-549a-82f5-76546ccf37c7" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527191 1074 "Let's rewrite the fraction $\\frac{3}{4}$ as a ratio:\n\n$$\n3:4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mixture has a ratio of $3$ gallons of paint to $4$ gallons of thinner." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527079 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527192 1069 "q-89850" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527192 1071 "![](images/e2/e21bce78ceda4767555bfd00bb6f0bf3171fd09e0ddabfe0290e3f2f1b9e7449.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527192 1072 #uuid "a5621f27-e279-5ddb-b2e9-fbd134010344" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527192 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph IV has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527119 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527193 1069 "q-159971" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527193 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Poisson random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-5\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527193 1072 #uuid "a6d182d3-6707-59a8-922c-7df2bb097cad" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527193 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-5\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-5+\\frac{4}{\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5+\\frac{4}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-5\\theta +4}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ -5\\theta +4 & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{4}{5}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527077 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527194 1069 "q-123620" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527194 1071 "How is the angle ${120}^{∘}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527194 1072 #uuid "a843ee14-f56b-58b5-bfd0-4708e1493281" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527194 1074 "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/72/72030fb9fe1e92b8be67d4a4cf2c899c83ba1242892231f18925c2354a143bdc.png)" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527023 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527195 1069 "q-146386" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527195 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0.8$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=2.2,$ calculate the covariance between $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527195 1072 #uuid "aa0cadd2-5104-5faa-b07c-30440395ac28" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527195 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0)(1)(0.1)+(0)(2)(0.2)+(0)(3)(0.3) \\\\  & +(2)(1)(0.1)+(2)(2)(0.2)+(2)(3)(0.1) \\\\  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.6-(0.8)(2.2) \\\\  & =-0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527029 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527196 1069 "q-139125" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527196 1071 "The displacement $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your final answer to the nearest Newton." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527196 1072 #uuid "ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527196 1074 "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot ⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18t,\\,36{t}^{2}-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =⟨18(4),\\,36({4}^{2})-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨72,\\,570⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(4)| & =\\sqrt{(72)^{2}+{(570)}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈575\\,\\text{N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest Newton." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527104 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527197 1069 "q-5436" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527197 1071 "Find the  $11$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $19$th term is $99$ and the $4$th term is $-6.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527197 1072 #uuid "ae437285-daa1-5ee1-861c-3f25e99e891f" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527197 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{19}=99$ and ${a}_{4}=-6$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{19}-{a}_{4}}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{99-(-6)}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{15} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{4}=-6,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{4}$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+3\\cdot 7 \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-27+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7n-34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $11$th term by substituting $n=11$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & =7(11)-34 \\\\  & =77-34 \\\\  & =43\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527066 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527198 1069 "q-148331" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527198 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=0.1,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=0.7,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.2.$ Find $\\text{Var}[7X-4Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527198 1072 #uuid "af828237-c44f-5a8f-92ae-a55b1aff2b6f" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527198 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[7X-4Y] & ={7}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.7+2\\cdot 7\\cdot (-4)\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4.9+11.2-11.2 \\\\  & =4.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527061 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527199 1069 "q-99437" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527199 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527199 1072 #uuid "b06c7090-b9ea-594c-b431-3fe9296b347e" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527199 1074 "To work out \n${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2},$\n we multiply \n$\\frac{1}{9}$\n by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{1}{81}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527064 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527200 1069 "q-159965" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527200 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ),\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527200 1072 #uuid "b3abf1cc-eadf-582f-929d-006fffbd1ae1" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527200 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{2}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{2}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{2}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{6(\\theta -1)+2\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 6(\\theta -1)+2\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 8\\theta -6 & =0 \\\\ 8\\theta  & =6 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527088 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527201 1069 "q-159318" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527201 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527201 1072 #uuid "b3af2400-05b8-5370-91fa-551e055c25be" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527201 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\text{ or }y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}, & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527057 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527202 1069 "q-146309" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527202 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the covariance between $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527202 1072 #uuid "b5d80cd0-76ca-5260-b15c-cf7c6e9faf1e" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527202 1074 "The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle \n\n$$\nS=[0,2]\\times [0,4].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $h(y)=\\frac{y}{8}.$ \n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527053 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527203 1069 "q-123640" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527203 1071 "![](images/ca/caacb6ef264b8f7862a893b4127704e46e66d33a212b53017e8bde4e32fa3004.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{2}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527203 1072 #uuid "ba6955a5-f1b2-5bb6-bbb0-ee805b7e5b79" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527203 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/43/432dbcd0967ba40a5f477d30c8e9b0985c8151fb2f13e54ad346cff4ac20c1c5.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={30}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527020 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527204 1069 "q-34277" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527204 1071 "![](images/1b/1bc441dcf36de00672be1ac0ff5004e25ffb04d64788a86df3c849da0600794a.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-\\frac{5}{4},-\\frac{5}{4}),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527204 1072 #uuid "ba6b8db9-c5f9-50b9-8e8a-d50f12658a32" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527204 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](images/e3/e3abf66be20a5ab3ed5d0b8812b127d6336cb88f8369937d415c8918d020e45e.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{5}{4})}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={45}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={225}^{∘}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527103 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527205 1069 "q-148307" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527205 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{8},$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=\\frac{3}{4},$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\frac{5}{16}.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X+Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527205 1072 #uuid "bade78ae-2b02-5df2-9480-36d57f4806a3" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527205 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X\\pm Y]=\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]\\pm 2\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X+Y]=\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{16}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527043 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527206 1069 "q-342248" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527206 1071 "Find the exact value of the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x$ whose tangent has a slope of $0.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527206 1072 #uuid "bc8ddc72-3ceb-5b31-bf69-44ea85d72dfb" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527206 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $0$ at the point where $x=2.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527106 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527207 1069 "q-101757" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527207 1071 "What are the like terms in the expression $10x-18y-7z+10?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527207 1072 #uuid "c03bfbc1-ac68-5596-a4f4-f714ecc885a9" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527207 1074 "We write the terms of the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n(10x)+(-18y)+(-7z)+(10).\n$$\n\nNone of these terms have the same variable part. Consequently, there are no like terms in this expression." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527092 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527208 1069 "q-148304" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527208 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=0.64,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=2.56,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.48.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X+Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527208 1072 #uuid "c3e39928-eadb-5eb3-9eda-f3457f0505b4" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527208 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X\\pm Y]=\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]\\pm 2\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X+Y]=0.64+2.56+2\\cdot 0.48=4.16.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527116 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527209 1069 "q-10685" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527209 1071 "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=3{x}^{4}+12{x}^{2}+2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(1,17)?$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527209 1072 #uuid "c428807e-3362-57cf-8f18-309a6336affe" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527209 1074 "This time, we'll use $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+12{x}^{2}+2{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{x}^{3}+2\\cdot 12x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}+24x+{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =12(1)^{3}+24(1)+(1)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =12+24+1 \\\\  & =37.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $37.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527047 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527210 1069 "q-148329" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527210 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=3,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=5,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=1.$ Find $\\text{Var}[3X-4Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527210 1072 #uuid "d139a777-1244-5988-9682-4e7da954d933" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527210 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-4Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =27+80-24 \\\\  & =83.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527085 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527211 1069 "q-18336" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527211 1071 "![](images/04/04cb5fcb2ea4013f64d76439a882730455f77908c9e885f52f262efce7bccb58.png)\n\nWhat's the equation of the above curve?" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527211 1072 #uuid "d3b4690a-a388-56c8-b51f-66b065fce082" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527211 1074 "This is the curve $y={x}^{3}$ that's been translated $1$ unit *down*.\n\n![](images/18/1827cffb455aeea03512c208f1cd2914ce669abc62a27dd56cb52c29bf8094c4.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y={x}^{3}\\,-\\,1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527114 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527212 1069 "q-159940" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527212 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent exponential random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -7.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527212 1072 #uuid "d69af2de-f48e-5047-98b1-6977b8b554d5" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527212 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -7.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\theta }-7.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\theta }-7.8 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\theta } & =7.8 \\\\ 4 & =7.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.513\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.513,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527120 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527213 1069 "q-3601" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527213 1071 "Factor $1-{j}^{3/2}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527213 1072 #uuid "d6fb18a1-0afa-52ee-a564-35b978a84f8b" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527213 1074 "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\n1-{j}^{3/2}={1}^{3}-{({j}^{1/2})}^{3}={1}^{3}-{(\\sqrt{j})}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the given expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{j}^{3/2} & ={1}^{3}-{(\\sqrt{j})}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-\\sqrt{j})({1}^{2}+1\\cdot \\sqrt{j}+(\\sqrt{j})^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\sqrt{j})(1+\\sqrt{j}+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527055 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527214 1069 "q-159942" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527214 1071 "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-3.54+2.2\\text{ }\\,\\theta -3\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527214 1072 #uuid "d834043c-817d-50e2-98ca-784bf169d161" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527214 1074 "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-3.54+2.2\\text{ }\\,\\theta -3\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =2.2-6\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.2-6\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 6\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =2.2 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.367\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.367,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527076 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527215 1069 "q-123891" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527215 1071 "![](images/08/08ee9a92a96f2ab0e4644dc02dfd4be605e72e9b778f54bf60d8e4014d58a7bd.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-3\\sqrt{3},9)$, calculate the angle $\\theta .$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527215 1072 #uuid "d86e19a0-8ae0-5dcf-aad3-3a9ae2b60335" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527215 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is supplementary to $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/e0/e0643cdb2d864b3af6466e24ab815dc8085e811dfdb86cdce253981a521faf82.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{9}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\theta +{\\theta }^{′}=\\pi ,$ then $\\theta =\\pi -{\\theta }^{′}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527073 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527216 1069 "q-276204" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527216 1071 "![](images/d2/d20da3acdbbf1f08a5c0713ce2fe0313a345319accfa1b18efbc87f46705faa8.png)\n\nThe graph above is a translation of $y={x}^{3}.$ Identify the equation that generated the graph.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527216 1072 #uuid "d93e70df-0d5a-518e-ab33-73b00d22f653" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527216 1074 "This is the curve $y={x}^{3}$ that's been translated $6$ units *up*.\n\n![](images/07/078aa5c391829a1544d0f83c51c32e0653312f2b06158200ffc82dd8cd784330.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y={x}^{3}+6.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527050 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527217 1069 "q-143850" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527217 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[4(X-1)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527217 1072 #uuid "da99206a-ee96-54b9-a8bb-1c95438c5559" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527217 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=4(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/bd/bd8d2d36a0fe570e6ccdb096c6c39bc9c9edeed9b008dada504801025055438f.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4(X-1)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}4(x-1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(x-1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}4(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1){\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)[y]_{y=1}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)({x}^{2}-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =3[(4-\\frac{8}{3}-2+2)-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)] \\\\  & =3(\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527052 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527218 1069 "q-342102" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527218 1071 "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=2{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+24{x}^{2}-16$ at the point where $x=3.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527218 1072 #uuid "def70879-cf84-5b54-bdb7-d96481c22cc6" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527218 1074 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+24{x}^{2}-16.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(3).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+24{x}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5{x}^{4}-8\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+24\\cdot 2x-0 \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}-32{x}^{3}+48x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =10({3}^{4})-32({3}^{3})+48(3) \\\\  & =810-864+144 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(3)=90.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527068 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527219 1069 "q-148399" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527219 1071 "For three random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3},$ we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]=3,\\,\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]=0.5,\\,\\text{Var}[{X}_{3}]=1.5\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=-0.75,\\,\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]=-3.5,\\,\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]=4\\,\n$$\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}+6{X}_{3}].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527219 1072 #uuid "e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527219 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n}$ are random variables, and ${a}_{1},…,{a}_{n}$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{X}_{i}]={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}]+2{\\sum }_{i<j}{a}_{i}{a}_{j}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].\n$$\n\nHence, for $n=3$ random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3}] & ={a}_{1}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+{a}_{2}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+{a}_{3}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{3}] \\\\  & +2{a}_{1}{a}_{2}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]+2{a}_{1}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]+2{a}_{2}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}+6{X}_{3}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{6}^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & +2\\cdot 1\\cdot 4\\cdot (-0.75)+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 6\\cdot (-3.5)+2\\cdot 4\\cdot 6\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+8+54-6-42+192 \\\\  & =209.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527118 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527220 1069 "q-186120" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527220 1071 "$1.2-5=${{field-1}}" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527220 1072 #uuid "e12935f7-7208-5953-a547-9693833e1308" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527220 1074 "Since $5$ is larger than $1.2,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem, which is to carry out the subtraction in the reverse order:\n\n$$\n5-1.2=3.8\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1.2-5=-3.8.\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527035 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527221 1069 "q-159323" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527221 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527221 1072 #uuid "e1859419-9341-5ec9-877c-8ef2835f890f" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527221 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 2$ and $y\\ge 2,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}})(1-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{128}{{x}^{4}{y}^{3}}, & x\\ge 2,\\,y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527081 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527222 1069 "q-147965" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527222 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.11$ | $0.44$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$\n\n*Hint: It can be shown that $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible values of $x$ and $y.$*" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527222 1072 #uuid "e6e3c2bf-143f-5ac7-a0ed-5faf8562286b" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527222 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\nP(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values of $x$ and $y.$ Therefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent. \n\nSince $X$ are $Y$ are independent, we have $\\text{Cov}(X,Y)=0.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527045 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527223 1069 "q-67315" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527223 1071 "The second and third terms of an arithmetic sequence are $45$ and $80,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527223 1072 #uuid "e88dff2d-42e3-57bd-b14e-035af9fb2260" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527223 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{2}=45$ and ${a}_{3}=80$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{3}-{a}_{2} \\\\  & =80-45 \\\\  & =35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{2}=45,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{2}$ is $1$ jump after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+1\\cdot d \\\\ 45 & ={a}_{1}+35 \\\\ 45-35 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 35 \\\\  & =10+35n-35 \\\\  & =35n-25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527111 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527224 1069 "q-148348" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527224 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X-2Y].$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527224 1072 #uuid "e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527224 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.25$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.55+2\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.55+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =1.8-{0.9}^{2} \\\\  & =0.99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.5-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+2\\cdot 1\\cdot f(2,1)+2\\cdot 2\\cdot f(2,2) \\\\  & =0+0+2\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.4-0.9\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-2Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 0.99+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =8.91+1-0.6 \\\\  & =9.31.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527071 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527225 1069 "q-112336" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527225 1071 "Factor $\\frac{{u}^{3}}{8}-1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527225 1072 #uuid "ee3cc387-c8ba-5e21-b6bf-54f77512a2c2" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527225 1074 "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{u}^{3}}{8}-1={(\\frac{u}{2})}^{3}-{1}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{3}}{8}-1 & ={(\\frac{u}{2})}^{3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{u}{2}-1)({(\\frac{u}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{u}{2}\\cdot 1+{(1)}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{u}{2}-1)(\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{u}{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527099 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527226 1069 "q-112333" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527226 1071 "Factor $\\frac{8}{{w}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{9}}.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527226 1072 #uuid "efa83c70-ebda-58c7-9dc4-66acbcb8874b" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527226 1074 "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a difference of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{{w}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{9}}={(\\frac{2}{w})}^{3}-{(\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{w}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{9}} & ={(\\frac{2}{w})}^{3}-{(\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{w}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})({(\\frac{2}{w})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{w}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{z}^{3}}+{(\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{w}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})(\\frac{4}{{w}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{w{z}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{({z}^{3})^{2}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{w}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{3}})(\\frac{4}{{w}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{w{z}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{z}^{6}})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527069 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527227 1069 "q-143879" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527227 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527227 1072 #uuid "f125a564-9e7e-50c1-b77c-f6cbd0ae6169" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527227 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{y{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{y}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+2-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527025 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527228 1069 "q-159331" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527228 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>2 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527228 1072 #uuid "f97149b0-2afa-55ad-bcc3-a7b6ba9e10a4" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527228 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{4{x}^{2}({y}^{2}-1)}{3{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3{y}^{3}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16x}{3{y}^{3}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527108 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527229 1069 "q-159315" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527229 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527229 1072 #uuid "fcac8514-54e9-5652-a542-ae9e241a741a" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527229 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527230 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527284 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527230 1062 #uuid "09565298-9c43-54e4-9ad2-16dc8176d4eb" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527230 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527195 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527231 1062 #uuid "106e19b3-24d9-5a35-a92b-54bd6690b59f" 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [17592186527231 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527206 13194139535762 17592186045417 true] [1759218652723